{
  "title": "Expand ingredient categorizer keyword coverage",
  "description": "data/ingredients.json has 640 keyword-to-category mappings. \
Measured against data/unique-ingredients.txt (16,411 historical parsed \
ingredient strings), ~10.7% of items fall through to 'Other'. The misses split \
into two buckets:\n\
\n\
1. Genuine vocabulary gaps: marshmallows, dulce de leche, pie filling, \
applesauce, candy melting wafers, chili beans, simple syrup, plain 'water', \
chocolate morsels, Heath bars, etc.\n\
2. Ingredient-parsing junk that no keyword list can fix ('+ 2 Tbsp warm \
water', '(440-gram) cans', ')'). Don't try to fix these with keywords — file \
separate parser issues if a pattern is common.\n\
\n\
Task: mine the most frequent 'Other' items and add keywords to \
data/ingredients.json until the Other rate is substantially down \
(frequency-weighted). To reproduce the measurement, run the categorizer \
(ramekin-core/src/ingredient_categorizer.rs) over the corpus and tally \
categories.\n\
\n\
Notes:\n\
- The matcher is longest-keyword-first token matching with plural inflection \
(s/es/ies/y), so prefer the shortest distinctive keyword and watch for \
collisions with existing longer keywords (e.g. 'dried basil' vs 'basil').\n\
- If p2-shopping-list-category-golden-set-from-prod has landed, use its \
prod-derived corpus and golden set as the primary target instead of \
unique-ingredients.txt, and make sure the golden test passes.\n\
- Keep the existing unit tests in ingredient_categorizer.rs green; add tests \
for newly handled tricky cases.",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 2,
